Myth: In the age before memory, there lived two weavers in neighboring villages—Astrid of the North and Somnia of the South. Both possessed extraordinary gifts, yet neither knew of the other's existence.

Astrid wove with threads of pure light, creating tapestries that illuminated the darkest nights. Her work was bold and visible, hanging in the sky for all to see. But she grew lonely, for her art could only be witnessed in waking hours, and people slept through her greatest displays.

Somnia wove with threads of shadow and starlight, creating visions that lived only in sleep. Her work was intimate and hidden, touching each person privately. But she too felt isolated, for no one could share their dreams or know that others dreamed as well.

One twilight, at the boundary between their lands, the two weavers met. Astrid showed Somnia how to weave light that could be seen. Somnia taught Astrid how to weave meanings that could be felt.

Together, they created a new art: the aurora that dances between earth and sky, visible to waking eyes yet dreamlike in its movement. Now their combined gift reminds us that the most beautiful truths exist in the space between—between light and dark, waking and sleeping, the seen and the felt.
