Myth: In the age when silence ruled the void, there existed a dreamer named Morpheus who wandered through emptiness, searching for meaning. Unlike the weaver Somnia who created the world's fabric, Morpheus possessed nothing—no loom, no threads, only the capacity to imagine.

For eons, Morpheus drifted alone, conjuring visions that dissolved the moment they formed. He imagined great forests that vanished like smoke, oceans that evaporated before making sound, creatures that flickered out of existence before drawing breath.

In his despair, Morpheus finally stopped moving. He closed his eyes and surrendered to the void. In that moment of absolute stillness, something extraordinary happened: his visions began to solidify. The images he had abandoned, believing them lost forever, had been quietly accumulating in the darkness, waiting.

They rushed toward him like a river finding its course—all his forgotten dreams crystallizing into form. Mountains rose, stars ignited, and life stirred.

Morpheus understood then that creation requires not just imagination, but release. He had been clinging too tightly to his visions, preventing them from becoming real.

